## Files

- `nanoforge.config.ts`: the app config that `nf` reads (`type: "<%= part %>"`). Fields and defaults:
  https://docs.nanoforge.eu/docs/cli/configuration/configuration-file
- `src/main.ts`: the entry file. The loader calls its exported `main(options)` from the bundled
  `main.js`, so keep the file name and the export.
- `src/components/`, `src/systems/`: one component or system per file,
  `<kebab-name>.component.ts` and `<kebab-name>.system.ts`.
- `assets/`: copied to the root of `dist/` on each build, so `assets/hero.png` is served as
  `/hero.png`. Read it with `ctx.assets.getAsset("/hero.png")`, an `NfFile` whose `path` is its URL.
- `dist/` is the build output, emptied on every build. `.nanoforge/` belongs to the NanoForge
  tools: don't edit it.

## Commands

`nf` comes from the `@nanoforge-dev/cli` dev dependency: run it as `<%= nf %> <command>`.
<% if (workspace) { %>
Run `nf dev` and `nf start` from the workspace root: they run this app with the other one, and
they read the `.env` of their `-d` folder (the current folder by default). To build this app
alone, run `<%= nf %> build -d apps/<%= part %>` at the root.
<% } else { %>
- `nf dev`: runs `nf build --watch` and `nf start --watch` together.<% if (part === "client") { %> The game is on
  http://localhost:3000 (`port` in the config).<% } %>
- `nf build`: bundles `src/main.ts` with Bun into `dist/`. It does not type-check.
- `nf start`: <% if (part === "client") { %>serves<% } else { %>runs<% } %> the last build.
<% } %>
<% if (workspace && packageManager === "npm") { %>Add a package with `npm install <package> -w apps/<%= part %>` at the workspace root.<% } else { %>Add a package with `<%= add %> <package>` in this folder.<% } %>

The `@nanoforge-dev/*` packages are on `^2`. The online docs follow the engine's development
branch and can describe APIs that the installed release lacks: the installed types
(`@nanoforge-dev/<package>/dist` in `node_modules`) are the reference.

## Game code

- `main` creates the app with `NanoforgeFactory.<%= part === "client" ? "createClient" : "createServer" %>`, registers each library with
  `app.use(...)`, then calls `await app.init(options)`. `app.use` throws after `init`, and
  `ecs.registry` throws before it. `await app.run()` starts the tick loop and returns.
- Components are plain classes. The registry keys them by their `name` field: give each class a
  `name` equal to its class name (the generated ones use `this.constructor.name`), and never store
  game data in `name`. Names must be unique in the app, and `entity` and `id` are reserved. An
  entity has one component per class: adding a second one replaces the first.
- Systems are `(registry, ctx) => void` functions. They run once per tick, in the order of the
  `registry.addSystem` calls. Their result is ignored, so an async system is not awaited.
- `registry.getZipper([A, B])` returns one object per entity that has both, keyed by component
  name: `({ A, B }) => …`. The values are the live components: change them in place.
  `getIndexedZipper` adds the entity `id`, and `registry.entityFromIndex(id)` turns it back into an
  entity for `killEntity` or `removeComponent`.
- `ctx.app.delta` is the time since the last tick, in milliseconds. `tickRate` in `main` is the
  number of ticks per second. `ctx.app.requestStop()` stops the game.<% if (part === "client") { %>
- Graphics use Konva: `@nanoforge-dev/graphics-2d` re-exports its shapes (`Circle`, `Rect`,
  `Layer`…), and `ctx.graphics.baseLayer` is a layer already on the stage. Coordinates are in a
  1920×1080 design space scaled to the window (the `viewport` option of `createClient`).
- Keys: `ctx.input.isKeyPressed(InputEnum.KeyW)`, with `InputEnum` from `@nanoforge-dev/input`. Its
  values are `KeyboardEvent.code` names.<% } %>
<% if (networked) { %>
## Network

- The client and the server connect over TCP, a WebSocket, and over UDP, a WebRTC data channel
  set up through a WebSocket. Their address and ports are `NANOFORGE_*` variables, which `nf start`
  reads from the `.env` of <%= workspace ? "the workspace root" : "its `-d` folder" %>. It gives
  `NANOFORGE_CLIENT_X` to the client and `NANOFORGE_SERVER_X` to the server, as `X` in
  `options.env`. Everything the client gets reaches the browser: never put a secret there.
  https://docs.nanoforge.eu/docs/cli/configuration/environment-variables
- The generated systems use `ctx.network.tcp` and encode JSON with `TextEncoder`. Check the
  installed `@nanoforge-dev/network` types before using another network API.
<% } %>
## Example

A component and a system, as the rules above want them:

```ts
// src/components/velocity-2d.component.ts
/** Speed of an entity, in pixels per second. */
export class Velocity2D {
  name = "Velocity2D";

  constructor(
    public x = 0,
    public y = 0,
  ) {}
}

// src/systems/apply-velocity-2d.system.ts
import { type Context } from "@nanoforge-dev/common";
import { type Registry } from "@nanoforge-dev/ecs";

import { Position2D } from "../components/position-2d.component";
import { Velocity2D } from "../components/velocity-2d.component";

/** Moves every entity that has a position and a velocity. */
export const applyVelocity2D = (registry: Registry, ctx: Context): void => {
  const seconds = ctx.app.delta / 1000;

  registry.getZipper([Position2D, Velocity2D]).forEach(({ Position2D, Velocity2D }) => {
    Position2D.x += Velocity2D.x * seconds;
    Position2D.y += Velocity2D.y * seconds;
  });
};
```

Register it in `main` with `registry.addSystem(applyVelocity2D)`.

